lemmas.proof_markdown:
A deleted point has at most $31648-1=31647$ current incident edges, and a deleted line has at most $38485-1=38484$ current incident edges. There are $147457^2$ points and $147457\cdot147458$ affine lines. Thus the normalized deletion charge is at most

$$
\lambda_0
=
\frac{31647\cdot147457^2+38484\cdot147457\cdot147458}
{147457^2\cdot147458}
=
\frac{147457(31648-1)+147458(38485-1)}
{147457\cdot147458}
=
\frac{10341345351}{21743714306}.
$$

When the deletion process terminates, every surviving point has degree at least $31648$ and every surviving line has degree at least $38485$, giving $G_0$.

nodes.statement_markdown:
For every $(k,r)$, construct an explicitly enumerated finite set $\Theta_{k,r}$ and integer systems $\Phi_\theta(k,r,z)$ such that $$\operatorname{BadIP}(k,r)\text{ feasible}
\Longrightarrow
\bigvee_{\theta\in\Theta_{k,r}}\exists z\in\mathbb Z^{s_\theta}:\Phi_\theta(k,r,z).$$

For each $(k,r)$, a deterministic generator produces a canonical pseudo-Boolean or CNF formula $F_{k,r}$ satisfying $$\operatorname{BadIP}(k,r)\text{ feasible}
\Longrightarrow
\operatorname{SAT}(F_{k,r}).$$

For every formula $F$, certificate $\pi$, closed exact-arithmetic proposition $s$, and arithmetic certificate $\alpha$, $$\operatorname{Check}_{\mathrm{unsat}}(F,\pi)=\mathrm{true}
\Longrightarrow\neg\operatorname{SAT}(F),$$ and $$\operatorname{Check}_{\mathbb Z}(s,\alpha)=\mathrm{true}
\Longrightarrow s.$$
